::JOUR_U01_L04_Q01::MLA.JOUR.MED.08
Which statement best describes media design in journalism? {
~It is only decoration and has no effect on meaning.#Incorrect. Design affects what readers notice and understand.
=It is the visual organization of journalism, including images, layout, captions, charts, and spacing.#Correct. Media design shapes how information is presented.
~It is a private note that readers never see.#Incorrect. Readers directly experience media design.
~It is a way to avoid accuracy rules.#Incorrect. Design must support accurate communication.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q02::MLA.JOUR.DIG.03
A photo crop shows only a small crowded group, while the full hallway was mostly open. What is the accuracy risk? {
~The crop gives too much context.#Incorrect. The issue is missing context.
~The crop makes the caption unnecessary.#Incorrect. Captions still matter.
=The crop may make readers believe the whole hallway was crowded.#Correct. A misleading crop can distort audience understanding.
~The crop proves the event did not happen.#Incorrect. The event happened, but the visual representation may mislead.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q03::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
Which caption best follows journalism format rules? {
=Students wait near the auditorium entrance before the winter concert on Friday.#Correct. This caption identifies action, place, and context clearly.
~This was the most amazing crowd ever.#Incorrect. This is opinion, not a factual caption.
~People doing stuff somewhere.#Incorrect. This is too vague for a useful caption.
~Everyone in the school was clearly there.#Incorrect. This may overstate what the photo proves.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q04::MLA.JOUR.MED.08
Why might a journalist use a mobile-friendly vertical layout? {
~To make facts harder to find#Incorrect. Good layout should improve access.
~To remove all visual context#Incorrect. Mobile layout can still include context.
~To make every story look like an advertisement#Incorrect. Journalism design should fit journalism purpose.
=To match how many readers view stories on phones#Correct. Design trends often reflect audience platform habits.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q05::MLA.JOUR.MED.08
Which design choice could exaggerate the importance of a routine story? {
~A clear caption under the image#Incorrect. Clear captions support understanding.
=A giant breaking-news headline for a routine club meeting#Correct. Oversized emphasis can misrepresent importance.
~A readable paragraph layout#Incorrect. Readability supports clarity.
~A properly labeled chart#Incorrect. Labels help accuracy.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q06::MLA.JOUR.DIG.03
What does visual accuracy require? {
=Images and visual information should represent the event or data without misleading the audience.#Correct. Visual accuracy protects trust.
~Images should always be cropped as tightly as possible.#Incorrect. Cropping should preserve important context.
~Charts should hide their scales.#Incorrect. Scales should be clear.
~Captions should avoid naming what appears.#Incorrect. Captions should identify and explain images.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q07::MLA.JOUR.MED.08
What is visual culture? {
~Only paintings in a museum#Incorrect. Visual culture includes broader image and design habits.
~A rule that journalism cannot use images#Incorrect. Journalism often uses images.
=The way people use, expect, and understand images and design in daily life#Correct. Visual culture shapes media design choices.
~A list of grammar rules#Incorrect. Visual culture is about image and design meaning.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q08::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
Which design choice best follows format-specific quality for a news graphic? {
~Use numbers without labels so readers must guess.#Incorrect. Labels are needed for clarity.
~Choose colors that hide key information.#Incorrect. Design should make information easier to understand.
~Make a small difference look huge by cutting off the scale.#Incorrect. This can mislead readers.
=Use a clear title, accurate labels, and a scale that represents the data honestly.#Correct. This supports quality and accuracy.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q09::MLA.JOUR.MED.08
What should a journalist ask after noticing a design choice? {
=What does this design make the audience notice first?#Correct. Audience attention is central to evaluating design.
~How can this design hide the main fact?#Incorrect. Design should not hide important facts.
~Can I remove all context?#Incorrect. Context helps accuracy.
~Will this make opinion look like news?#Incorrect. That is a risk to avoid, not a goal.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q10::MLA.JOUR.DIG.03
Which situation most clearly violates accurate visual communication? {
~A photo includes an accurate caption.#Incorrect. Accurate captions support visual communication.
=A chart changes scale to make a tiny difference look large.#Correct. Misleading scale distorts visual information.
~A mobile story card has a readable headline.#Incorrect. Readability supports access.
~A layout places a caption beside the related photo.#Incorrect. That helps readers connect image and context.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q11::MLA.JOUR.MED.08
Why should design match a story's importance? {
~So all stories look like emergencies#Incorrect. That would mislead readers.
~So routine information is hidden#Incorrect. Design should clarify information.
=So the audience receives a visual signal that fits the actual news value#Correct. Design should not overstate or understate the story.
~So readers cannot tell opinion from news#Incorrect. Readers should be able to tell the format.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q12::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
An opinion illustration appears beside a news article without a label. What is the issue? {
~The illustration is too accurate.#Incorrect. Accuracy is not the problem.
~The story has too many facts.#Incorrect. Facts are expected in news.
~The page has a caption.#Incorrect. Captions can help clarity.
=The format may confuse readers by making opinion look like straight news.#Correct. Format labels help readers understand the purpose.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q13::MLA.JOUR.MED.08
Which choice best reflects a current digital design trend? {
=Short text blocks, strong image cards, and layouts that work on small screens#Correct. These features reflect digital and mobile visual habits.
~Unreadable type and unlabeled images#Incorrect. Good design should improve access.
~No attention to platform or audience#Incorrect. Platform and audience shape design choices.
~Charts without numbers#Incorrect. Data visuals need clear information.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q14::MLA.JOUR.DIG.03
What should a journalist do before sharing a visual image? {
~Assume the image is accurate if it looks dramatic.#Incorrect. Drama does not prove accuracy.
=Check that the image and caption represent the event accurately.#Correct. Visual accuracy requires verification and context.
~Crop out important context to make it more exciting.#Incorrect. That may mislead the audience.
~Remove all identifying information, even when it is needed for clarity.#Incorrect. Identification may be necessary and should be accurate.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q15::MLA.JOUR.MED.08
Which answer best evaluates a photo crop? {
~It is good because it is close-up, no matter what it hides.#Incorrect. Close-up crops can be useful or misleading depending on context.
~It is bad because all crops are dishonest.#Incorrect. Cropping is not automatically dishonest.
=It is appropriate if it focuses attention without removing context needed for accurate understanding.#Correct. This balances design and accuracy.
~It is appropriate only if there is no caption.#Incorrect. Captions often support accuracy.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q16::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
Why do accepted format rules matter in visual journalism? {
~They let designers ignore the facts.#Incorrect. Format rules support accurate communication.
~They require every image to be black and white.#Incorrect. Format rules do not require one visual style.
~They remove the need for audience awareness.#Incorrect. Format rules are connected to audience needs.
=They help the visual format communicate clearly and responsibly.#Correct. Format rules guide quality work.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q17::MLA.JOUR.MED.08
Which design choice helps readers connect image and story? {
=A concise caption placed directly with the related photo#Correct. This gives readers context at the point of need.
~A caption placed several unrelated stories away#Incorrect. Separation can confuse readers.
~A photo with no identification#Incorrect. Lack of identification weakens context.
~A headline that contradicts the image#Incorrect. Contradiction weakens clarity.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q18::MLA.JOUR.DIG.03
Which example shows responsible use of digital equipment for journalism? {
~Editing an image to make the event look more crowded than it was#Incorrect. That distorts visual accuracy.
~Sharing a blurry image with no context because it is fast#Incorrect. Speed should not replace clarity.
=Recording a clear image of the event and pairing it with accurate identifying information#Correct. This supports accurate visual communication.
~Using a filter that changes what happened#Incorrect. Filters can mislead if they alter the facts.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q19::MLA.JOUR.MED.08
What is the strongest reason to study media design trends? {
~To copy every trend without thinking#Incorrect. Journalists should evaluate trends, not copy blindly.
=To understand how visual choices shape audience interpretation#Correct. Design affects meaning and audience understanding.
~To make facts less important#Incorrect. Facts remain essential.
~To avoid all images in journalism#Incorrect. Images are often useful when accurate.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q20::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
Which visual package best follows quality rules for a news story? {
~A dramatic image, no caption, and a headline that overstates the facts#Incorrect. This package can mislead.
~A chart with hidden numbers and no source label#Incorrect. This lacks clarity and accountability.
~A photo crop that removes the main context#Incorrect. Missing context can distort meaning.
=A relevant image, accurate caption, clear headline, and layout that matches the story purpose#Correct. This supports format-specific quality.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q21::MLA.JOUR.MED.08
Which question best checks audience effect? {
=What might readers understand or misunderstand because of this design?#Correct. This question directly evaluates audience interpretation.
~Can the design make the story less accurate?#Incorrect. That identifies risk, but the stronger question examines audience understanding broadly.
~Can I make the image more dramatic regardless of truth?#Incorrect. That is not a responsible design question.
~Can the caption be removed to save space?#Incorrect. Removing context may weaken accuracy.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q22::MLA.JOUR.DIG.03
Why is an unlabeled chart risky in journalism? {
~It always contains false numbers.#Incorrect. The numbers may be true but unclear.
~It makes readers too informed.#Incorrect. The risk is confusion, not too much understanding.
=Readers may not know what the numbers represent or how to interpret them.#Correct. Labels are essential for clear visual information.
~It proves the story is opinion.#Incorrect. Missing labels do not automatically make a story opinion.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q23::MLA.JOUR.MED.08
Which revision improves a misleading crowded-hallway photo? {
~Crop the image even tighter.#Incorrect. A tighter crop may increase the misleading effect.
=Use a wider crop or add a caption explaining the specific group shown.#Correct. This restores context and supports accuracy.
~Remove all captions and let readers guess.#Incorrect. Guessing weakens understanding.
~Use a headline claiming the entire hallway was packed.#Incorrect. That overstates what the image shows.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q24::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
Which statement best connects design to journalism format rules? {
~Design choices do not matter if the story has words.#Incorrect. Visual design can shape meaning.
~Design should make every format look identical.#Incorrect. Different formats have different needs.
~Design should hide whether a piece is news or opinion.#Incorrect. Format clarity matters.
=Design should support the purpose and accepted rules of the journalism format.#Correct. Quality work matches design to format and purpose.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_Q25::MLA.JOUR.MED.08
Which response best shows Lesson 4 mastery? {
=The crop attracts attention, but it removes context, so a wider image or accurate caption would better support visual accuracy.#Correct. This evaluates design, audience effect, accuracy, and revision.
~The crop is dramatic, so it is automatically good journalism.#Incorrect. Drama alone is not enough.
~The crop is a design choice, so accuracy does not matter.#Incorrect. Accuracy matters in visual journalism.
~The audience can figure everything out without context.#Incorrect. Journalists should provide clear context.
}
